title: The Chemnitz conference
subtitle: 21 October. The factory councils of Saxony are waiting for our call.
new-page: true
tags: kpd_event
view-if: year = 1923 and month = 10 and sub = 2 and (y23_saxony_done = 1 or zeigner = 0) and y23_chemnitz_done = 0
on-arrival: {! Q.y23_chemnitz_done = 1; window.KPD.zaRefresh(Q); !}

= The Chemnitz conference

The conference of Saxony's factory councils, about 450 delegates, meets in Chemnitz on 21 October. The Reichswehr is already moving: General Müller banned the Proletarian Hundreds on the 13th, and his troops enter Saxony on the 23rd. The KPD intends to call a general strike from Chemnitz as the signal for the German October, and Brandler is going to say so.

[? if zeigner = 1 : The SPD ministers in the Saxon cabinet are unwilling to call a general strike. ?][? if zeigner = 0 : Saxony has no workers' government to shelter the conference, and the SPD's leaders want no strike. ?] Whether a strike spreads beyond the Communist-led districts depends on the ground we have laid in the months before this conference: our organisation in Saxony, the joint actions with the SPD workers, and a second district ready to follow.

{!<div class="kpd-insert" data-insert="october"></div>!}

A rising is not yet available in this version of the game.

- @rising: Call the general strike and the armed rising. (Not implemented yet.)
- @strike: Call the general strike, but no rising.
- @calloff: Call it off. The party cannot win this fight.

@rising
choose-if: 1 = 0
unavailable-subtitle: Not implemented yet.
on-arrival: {!
if (Q.y23_unity >= 4) {
    Q.y23_outcome = 'rising_success';
    Q.civil_war = 1;
    Q.cw_red = 60;
    Q.cw_white = 95;
    Q.cw_month = 0;
} else {
    Q.y23_outcome = 'rising_crushed';
}
!}
go-to: rising_won if y23_outcome = 'rising_success'; rising_lost if y23_outcome = 'rising_crushed'

@rising_won
title: The German October begins

= The rising

The strike is called, and it spreads. The Hundreds take the police stations of Saxony, the SPD left stays at the side of the councils, and in the Ruhr and in Hamburg the workers stop. The Reichswehr marches and hesitates; the Freikorps of Bavaria muster to the south. For the first time since 1919 Germany is on the edge of a civil war, and this time the workers have shown they can fight.

The next months will decide it. We have no single decisive battle, only a campaign with fronts and the question of who, in Berlin, will command the Reichswehr.

- @root: To the campaign.

@rising_lost
title: The rising is crushed

= The rising is crushed

The call goes out and the response is a patchwork. In Saxony the Hundreds hold a few towns for days before the Reichswehr takes them. The SPD left withdraws its support, and the councils, which do not feel ready, stay at the machines. Moscow had a date and the party had a plan, and neither had the workers.

[? if y23_unity < 2 : The SPD left's refusal was fatal. ?][? if y23_unity >= 2 : The alliance with the SPD left held, but the other forces were not enough. ?]

Hundreds of workers are dead or arrested. The party is ruined, and Moscow will look for someone to blame.

- @root: Continue.

@strike
on-arrival: {!
window.KPD.refreshOctober(Q);
if (Q.oct_ok == 1) {
    Q.y23_outcome = 'strike_holds';
    Q.saxony_survives = 1;
    Q.workers_kpd += 4;
} else {
    Q.y23_outcome = 'strike_fails';
}
!}
go-to: strike_won if y23_outcome = 'strike_holds'; strike_lost if y23_outcome = 'strike_fails'

@strike_won
title: The general strike holds

= The strike holds

Saxony stops. The SPD left votes with the councils, Stresemann, with a divided cabinet and a Reichswehr that does not want to be called a civil-war army, negotiates rather than marching. The Saxon workers' government survives into the winter. It is not a revolution. It is a workers' government that has not been crushed, which in 1923 is a rare thing.

- @root: Continue.

@strike_lost
title: The strike fails

= The strike fails

The call is made and the strike does not leave the Communist districts. The Reichswehr enters Saxony on the 23rd, and the SPD ministers, who had never wanted this, leave the cabinet. Our own people are left to explain to the workers what the strike was for.

- @root: Continue.

@calloff
subtitle: [+ za_note +]
choose-if: za_lvl < 3
unavailable-subtitle: The Left holds the Zentralausschuss and will not let the party retreat.
on-arrival: {!
Q.kf_left_dissent += Q.za_cost;
Q.y23_outcome = 'called_off';
Q.comintern_trust -= 10;
Q.kf_left_dissent += 10;
Q.kf_right_dissent += 10;
Q.workers_kpd -= 3;
Q.y23_hamburg_pending = 1;
!}
go-to: root

We cancel the strike. The news cannot reach everyone, and the chairman of the conference withdraws his call to the Saxon councils. It is a retreat, and it is also a recognition that the German October exists in Moscow's calendar and not yet on Germany's streets. Brandler argued that the party could not win, and that he would be blamed for being right.
